e Supreme Court were  put into force with the aim of making  things equal for cohabiting couples and  married and civil partners and it was  just this requirement that there should  be a nomination form sent in that led to  miss Brewster not getting her survivors  pension and this was based around  article 14 of the ECHR one wonders  whether or not  given that there are some movements in  terms of a relationship with at the  European Court of Justice with the  European Union  this will continue yeah I thought it's  very interesting so under Article  fourteen there are various kinds of  discrimination there too outlawed and  marital status so being a cohabit ear a  thurr than a wife or a civil partner is  what this distinction was based on and  that's outlawed under Article fourteen  discrimination on the basis of marital  status and you're quite right if we were  ever to leave the European Convention on  Human Rights you wouldn't be able to  succeed in this type of case but  actually we just don't know what's going  to happen in regard to that I've I've  seen today just on the way here actually  that the Minister for brexit has  responded to a written question that was  about article three to do with  prohibition on torture and he said in  response to that that the government has  no plans to leave the European  Convention on Human Rights but I don't  think anyone's asked the government  specifically about article fourteen or  about article one protocol one which is  the right to enjoy your possessions  peacefully which was also in play here  because mr. McMullin had been paying  into his pension scheme for 15 years and  he'd been living with MS Brewster for  ten years and it was their right to his  property in his pension that was in  question her any I rather suspect given  the outcome of the case at the Supreme  Court people will now be asking the  government exactly those questions but  given the distress given that the  financial concerns of of taking a case  l